# Complex numbers

Euler's formula, De Moivre's theorem, roots of unity, and the change between rectangular form $a + bi$ and polar form $r e^{i\theta}$. The modulus $|z|$ is built in. Import with `![[std/complex.mpp]]`.

## Euler's formula and De Moivre

$$ e^{i x} \overset{?}{=} \cos x + i \sin x $$

$$ e^{i \pi / 2} \overset{?}{=} i $$

$$ \cos x \overset{?}{=} \frac{e^{i x} + e^{-i x}}{2} $$

$$ \sin x \overset{?}{=} \frac{e^{i x} - e^{-i x}}{2i} $$

De Moivre's theorem, $(\cos x + i \sin x)^n = \cos(nx) + i \sin(nx)$, for $n = 3$ and $n = 5$.

$$ (\cos x + i \sin x)^3 \overset{?}{=} \cos(3x) + i \sin(3x) $$

$$ (\cos x + i \sin x)^5 \overset{?}{=} \cos(5x) + i \sin(5x) $$

## Polar and rectangular form

The point at distance $r$ and angle $\theta$, as $a + bi$.

$$ z_{\text{rect}}(r, \theta) := r \cos\theta + i r \sin\theta $$

$$ z_{\text{rect}}(2, \tfrac{\pi}{2}) \overset{?}{=} 2i $$

$$ z_{\text{rect}}(\sqrt{2}, \tfrac{\pi}{4}) \overset{?}{=} 1 + i $$

The modulus of $a + bi$.

$$ r_{\text{mod}}(a, b) := \sqrt{a^2 + b^2} $$

$$ r_{\text{mod}}(3, 4) \overset{?}{=} 5 $$

$$ |3 + 4i| \overset{?}{=} r_{\text{mod}}(3, 4) $$

The argument of $a + bi$, in $(-\pi, \pi]$.

$$ \theta_{\text{arg}}(a, b) := \mathrm{atan2}(b, a) $$

$$ \theta_{\text{arg}}(1, 1) \overset{?}{=} \frac{\pi}{4} $$

$$ \theta_{\text{arg}}(-1, 0) \overset{?}{=} \pi $$

$$ z_{\text{rect}}(r_{\text{mod}}(1, \sqrt{3}), \theta_{\text{arg}}(1, \sqrt{3})) \overset{?}{=} 1 + i\sqrt{3} $$

Products multiply the moduli and add the arguments.

$$ |(1 + i)(\sqrt{3} + i)| \overset{?}{=} |1 + i| \cdot |\sqrt{3} + i| $$

$$ z_{\text{rect}}(2, \tfrac{\pi}{4}) z_{\text{rect}}(3, \tfrac{\pi}{6}) \overset{?}{=} z_{\text{rect}}(6, \tfrac{5\pi}{12}) $$

## Roots of unity

The $k$-th of the $n$ $n$-th roots of unity.

$$ \omega(k, n) := e^{2 \pi i k / n} $$

$$ \omega(1, 4) \overset{?}{=} i $$

$$ \omega(1, 5)^5 \overset{?}{=} 1 $$

$$ \omega(1, 3) \overset{?}{=} -\frac{1}{2} + \frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} i $$

The $n$-th roots of unity add to zero (for $n \ge 2$).

$$ \sum_{k=0}^{4} \omega(k, 5) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

$$ \sum_{k=0}^{5} \omega(k, 6) \overset{?}{=} 0 $$

## Contour integrals

Going once round the unit circle, $\oint dz/z = 2\pi i$; with $z = e^{it}$, $dz = i e^{it}\,dt$.

$$ \int_0^{2\pi} \frac{i e^{i t}}{e^{i t}} \, dt \overset{?}{=} 2 \pi i $$

**Theorem.** (Cauchy's integral formula) If $f$ is holomorphic on and inside a positively oriented simple closed contour $\gamma$ and $a$ lies inside $\gamma$, then $f(a) = \frac{1}{2\pi i} \oint_\gamma \frac{f(z)}{z - a}\,dz$. In particular $\oint_\gamma dz/z = 2\pi i$ for a contour enclosing 0.
